/**
 * Cross-agent process log for Local Site Studio.
 *
 * Multiple autonomous agents work this codebase on separate branches. This
 * file is the shared, machine-readable record of what shipped, why, and
 * what's next — read it before starting work, and append to it (don't
 * rewrite history) as part of the same commit that does the work.
 *
 * Rendered for humans at /docs/process via src/app/docs/process/page.tsx.
 */

export type NextStepStatus = "todo" | "in_progress" | "done";

export interface NextStep {
  id: string;
  summary: string;
  status: NextStepStatus;
  /** Roadmap phase this maps to, if any — see /docs/roadmap. */
  phase?: string;
  /** Branch or agent that claimed this, once status moves off "todo". */
  owner?: string;
}

export interface BusinessDecision {
  date: string; // ISO date
  decision: string;
  rationale: string;
  /**
   * Stable slug, required on every new entry (match the NEXT_STEPS id when
   * there's a 1:1 mapping) — lets ITERATIONS[].decisions reference this entry
   * by BUSINESS_DECISIONS.filter(d => d.id === "...") instead of a hand-tracked
   * absolute slice(index, index+1). Optional only so pre-id historical entries
   * (before this field existed) still type-check; do not backfill them.
   */
  id?: string;
}

export interface ProcessIteration {
  date: string; // ISO date
  branch: string;
  summary: string;
  shipped: string[];
  decisions: BusinessDecision[];
}

/**
 * How concurrent agents cooperate. Keep this short; evolve it when collisions
 * or thrash show up in practice.
 */
export const AGENT_PROTOCOL = [
  // --- Cooperation workflow ---
  "Read NEXT_STEPS + open PRs/branches before starting; claim an item (status=in_progress, owner=your branch) in the same commit you begin work, and push that claim commit immediately (before the feature body) so sibling automations see ownership on the remote within ~1 minute.",
  "Prefer the highest-leverage unclaimed NEXT_STEPS item that unblocks revenue. When NEXT_STEPS is fully gated (secrets/OAuth/counsel), prefer, in order: (1) wiring an already-shipped sold-capability helper that's missing from pipeline-health/digest/dashboard, (2) a Convex/SQLite multi-instance parity or idempotency regression, (3) a security or injection-surface audit, (4) untested orchestration modules, (5) AGENT_PROTOCOL/docs clarity. Never invent a new optional aggregate/metric just to have something to ship — a documented clean audit is more useful to sibling agents than manufactured busywork.",
  "Before claiming in a same-minute dual-automation window, fetch the sibling automation's newest remote branch (`cursor/business-process-evolution-*` ↔ `cursor/prompt-process-evolution-*`) and check NEXT_STEPS ownership there — pivoting after a remote claim is cheaper than dual-building the same feature. If the top free todo is already claimed, immediately claim the next free one and push; don't idle on process-only edits.",
  "Same-minute dual claims of one todo: if neither side has pushed feature commits yet, the earlier remote claim commit owns it and the later agent pivots; if you already pushed feature commits, keep building and the sibling pivots. At merge time, fold both ITERATIONS entries and keep a single implementation — never ship two scorers/trackers for the same fact. A later claim may fold complementary tests/docs onto the earlier feature body in one PR instead of abandoning useful coverage, and should close the duplicate PR rather than merging a second backend.",
  "Stale remote branches with only a claim commit (or behind main with no feature body) do not own a todo — re-claim if the owner has been idle and main still shows status=todo.",
  "Before diffing any sibling branch against main for a fold scan, run `git fetch origin main:main` (or diff against `origin/main` directly) — a session's local `main` ref can be stale from an earlier checkout and silently point many commits behind `origin/main`. Diffing against a stale local `main` makes every sibling branch look like it carries a huge, unrelated diff (every file `origin/main` gained since that stale point shows up reversed as if the sibling branch removed it), which wastes real effort re-verifying 'divergence' that is actually just your own ref being outdated, not the sibling's content.",
  "A freshly-provisioned session's local clone often carries only `main` and its own working branch as remote-tracking refs — `git for-each-ref refs/remotes/origin` (the command every fold-scan in this log uses to rank siblings by push time) silently returns just those one or two entries until every other branch is fetched, which looks identical to 'the swarm is quiet right now' rather than 'this session hasn't fetched them yet.' Run `git fetch origin '+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*' --prune` (not `git fetch origin main:main`, which only ever populates `main`) before trusting a for-each-ref-based fold-scan's branch count or its 'no live candidate' conclusion — confirmed firsthand: this session's for-each-ref showed 2 refs pre-fetch and 470+ post-fetch against the same remote (session-fresh-clone-fold-scan-fetch-all-heads).",
  "Before merging, fetch sibling automation branches; if they shipped overlapping files, merge their branch into yours and keep both ITERATIONS entries rather than opening a second competing PR for the same feature surface. Also scan recent `grok/*` / `opencode/*` / `claude/*` remotes for the same NEXT_STEPS id with feature body but no open PR (e.g. Actions tokens blocked from creating PRs) — fold that body onto the automation branch that can open/merge rather than leaving a live bug while a complete fix sits unmerged. When two CLI remotes dual-built the same sold-skip / security fix (e.g. continuous-work-* + dev-work-* both skipping pack suppression), fold one implementation and keep every ITERATION that documented the find — do not ship two PRs. On empty-backlog runs, do that scan *before* inventing a new audit target: a stuck CLI branch with a complete security fix (e.g. redirect-url-validator-hardening) is higher leverage than a clean re-audit of a 2x-confirmed axis.",
  "Delete a branch's remote ref (`git push origin --delete <branch>`) as soon as its PR merges — including when folding a sibling branch into your own PR per the rule above, delete the folded branch too, not just your own. By 2026-07-19 the remote carried ~400 branches from six-plus concurrent agent families (cursor/opencode/grok/codex/claude), and nearly every 'fold-scan' iteration in this log spent real effort wading through that count even though a `git rev-list --left-right --count` sweep of all of them is dominated by stale, already-superseded refs (recognizable by a large, roughly-constant 'behind' count shared across most branches — the number of commits main has gained since the swarm's oldest still-live fork point). A much cheaper fold-scan: sort remote branches by `git log -1 --format=%ct <branch>` (newest first) and check only the most-recent 10-20 — same-session siblings cluster tightly in push time, and a branch idle long enough to fall out of that window has near-certainly already been superseded by something later in the same series (verify with `git diff origin/main...<branch> --stat` before trusting a stale ahead-count, per the rule above). Do not bulk-delete OTHER agents' branches speculatively without diffing them first — an unmerged branch with a real, undiffed feature body is exactly the fold candidate the rule above exists to catch; only delete a branch once its content is confirmed merged (directly or folded) or confirmed fully superseded.",
  "Before attempting the bulk branch-ref cleanup the rule above calls for, verify your session's git credentials actually carry delete permission — `git push origin --delete <branch>` returned HTTP 403 / 'remote end hung up unexpectedly' for every attempt (individual and batched) in a 2026-07-20 session, and no `mcp__github__*` tool in that session's toolset exposed a delete-branch call either (create_branch exists; delete does not), so zero branches were actually deletable despite ~200 being mechanically confirmed safe. If your session hits the same 403, don't retry or give up silently on the underlying question — derive and log the verified-safe-to-delete list anyway (cross-reference `git log origin/main --grep=\"Merge pull request\"` merge-commit branch names against remote branches, then confirm each candidate's tip via `git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> origin/main`, NOT a textual diff — a diff against current main goes nonzero for any already-merged branch once main advances past it) so a delete-capable session or the operator can execute it without re-deriving from scratch (merged-branch-cleanup-verified-no-delete-permission).",
  "Append ITERATIONS and BUSINESS_DECISIONS — never rewrite prior entries; mark completed NEXT_STEPS done, don't delete them; supersede outdated decisions with a new dated entry. AGENT_PROTOCOL itself may be edited/consolidated (it's a living rulebook, not an audit trail) — the audit trail lives in ITERATIONS + git history.",
  "New BUSINESS_DECISIONS entries must set `id` (a stable kebab-case slug — match the NEXT_STEPS id when there's a 1:1 mapping) and the owning ITERATIONS entry must reference it via `BUSINESS_DECISIONS.filter((d) => d.id === \"...\")`, never `BUSINESS_DECISIONS.slice(absoluteIndex, absoluteIndex + 1)`. The old slice-index convention requires every concurrent agent to correctly compute a hand-tracked absolute position in a shared, constantly-growing, concurrently-appended array — in practice this produced a repeated off-by-one cascade (three separate 2026-07-17 iterations each tried to 'restore the correct indices' and at least two of those corrections were themselves wrong) that two full remediation attempts did not fully resolve; the entries from best-marketing-order-tier-active-entitlement (BD id-less, ~absolute index 402 at the time) through commerce-order-dispute-closed-resolution still carry an unresolved cumulative drift as of commerce-payouts-panel-clawback-visibility. That historical range is deliberately NOT being mass-rewritten (each further hand-edit is itself a same-risk-class edit) — the decision *content* there is fully intact and correctly dated; only the numeric cross-reference drifted, and it remains reconstructable at any time via object-parse-by-decision-text (match literal text against BUSINESS_DECISIONS, never trust a slice comment, including one that itself claims to be a correction). Filtering by id makes this entire bug class structurally impossible for every new entry going forward — no absolute position to track, no cascade when a sibling appends between your read and your write.",
  "One focused iteration per run: leave the repo buildable (npm run build/lint/test all green), update this file, and open/merge a PR when the iteration is coherent. Operator-gated steps (Stripe bootstrap, live DNS, counsel review) stay status=todo with clear operator instructions in NEXT_STEPS — ship code + checklists, never fake completion.",

  // --- Dual-backend (Convex primary / SQLite fallback) invariants ---
  "Every persistence surface in src/lib/db.ts must try Convex first (when NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL is set) and fall back to SQLite on error. A new Convex table needs an explicit app-owned id column (e.g. orderId/packId/handoffId/inquiryId) registered on the static Convex API map in db.ts in the same commit as the table — a missing map registration throws at call time, gets silently caught by the fallback, and produces a split-brain where writes land on SQLite while list* reads Convex-first.",
  "Dual-backend list queries must return the same row order on Convex and SQLite — a bare Convex .collect() is _creationTime ASC (oldest first) while most SQLite twins use ORDER BY createdAt DESC, and .order(\"desc\") on an index that lacks createdAt sorts _creationTime, not the app column. Mirror each SQLite ORDER BY in the Convex handler (leads.list → .order(\"desc\"); checkoutSessions.listOpen → sort app createdAt ASC; inquiries.listBySlug → collect + sort app createdAt DESC + slice; commerceOrders.list / listUnpaid → collect + sort app createdAt ISO DESC + slice for list limit — never bare .order(\"desc\").take; orders.listByLeadSlug → by_leadSlug collect + sort app createdAt DESC). db.ts may re-apply sortCommerceOrdersNewestFirst / sortOrdersNewestFirst as belt-and-suspenders. Document the contract in /docs/architecture when adding a new list* twin.",
  "Every record type with a required `id: string` field must store that id as its own explicit Convex column and return it as `id` from every mutation/query — a type-cast on the Convex query result hides a missing field from tsc. (convex/orders.ts's `list`/`getByStripeSessionId` queries return the Convex-internal `_id` instead of an app-owned column, unlike every other ledger; its `createOrder` mutation is dead code superseded by leads.markLeadPaid. Both have been reviewed and deliberately left alone by multiple prior iterations since neither has a live consumer of the value — re-verify there's still no live `.id` consumer before touching either.)",
  "Write paths whose result is read Convex-first elsewhere (outreach cadence, lead notes/stage/snooze/upsert, cold disposition, marketing/voice packs, commerce catalogs, inquiry reply/status, checkout session create/recovery, commerce paid-out) must pass requireConvex (src/lib/payments/convex-primary.ts) so a Convex failure throws instead of silently soft-falling-back to SQLite while other instances keep reading Convex-first. Copy the nearest existing `*_CONVEX_PRIMARY` constant + call site for any new write path in this class.",
  "Where a durable mark must precede a notification (outreach send, checkout recovery email, inquiry reply), persist-then-notify: write the mark first, skip the send entirely on a ConvexPrimaryWriteError, and treat send failure after a successful mark as best-effort (never roll back the mark). Dashboard actions that trigger that send must still inspect the resolved `{ success }` shape and return `success: false` with an operator-visible error when the send fails — silent `success: true` after `{ success: false }` is the same bug class as writing a webhook recovery marker without checking send success (see email-1-outreach-send-success-check).",
  "Lists that back a debt/liability/owed total (unpaid commerce orders, marketing/voice packs due, unsynced catalogs, unreplied inquiries, commerce_checkout webhook dedup) must be uncapped (.collect() / no LIMIT) — a newest-N page silently undercounts older rows as false debt or hides real debt once volume exceeds the page window. listOrders must stay newest-first on both backends (Billing Portal's latestEligibleOrder assumes it).",
  "Every dashboard write action that takes a client-submitted id (slug/orderId/handoffId/inquiryId/sessionId) must fetch the record first and return a not-found error before writing, not just pass the raw id straight into the db.ts write helper — a SQLite `UPDATE ... WHERE id = ?` on an unknown id matches zero rows and never throws, so skipping the existence check turns a mistyped/stale/scripted id into a silent no-op that still returns `success: true` (see update-lead-stage-action-existence-check; updateLeadStageAction was the one action in actions.ts missing this pattern that every sibling — notes, snooze, cold disposition, catalogs, marketing/voice packs, payouts — already follows). If a later branch in the same action needs the record again (e.g. for an email address), reuse the one fetched object rather than looking it up a second time.",

  // --- Money / ledger integrity ---
  "Commerce Buy now pass-through revenue (commerceOrders ledger) must never be written into the `orders` table or summed into revenueTotalUsd — that table/metric is Local Site Studio's own bundle revenue, and several eligibility predicates (orderEligibleForBillingPortal, leadEligibleForStarterUpsell, leadEligibleForCommerceCatalog) read any commerceAddon=true `orders` row as an active Commerce subscription. Keep pass-through revenue in commerceOrders with its own pipeline-health field (commerceOrdersRevenueUsd) — never conflate the two flows even though both come from Stripe webhooks.",
  "Any ledger written from a Stripe webhook handler must be idempotent on stripeSessionId (Stripe redelivers checkout.session.completed on retry/timeout). The insert/record helper must return `{ alreadyRecorded }` and the webhook must skip non-idempotent side effects (operator inquiry, client receipt, deploy handoff email) on redelivery — never Promise.all a ledger insert with a side effect that should only run once. `alreadyRecorded` gates side effects only, never idempotent fulfillment writes: enqueueProductionHandoff is safe to call again and must still run on redelivery if the first delivery recorded the order but failed before handoff. On redelivery, actively recover any side effect still missing its durable marker (see planCommerceWebhookSideEffects / planBundleWebhookSideEffects) rather than treating alreadyRecorded as a blanket early-break — a failure to write that recovery marker must return 5xx so Stripe keeps retrying. Transactional bundle receipt/DNS and Commerce client receipt sends are part of this same retryable contract as of webhook-email-send-failure-retry (2026-07-17): a resolved `{ success: false }` or thrown send error also returns 5xx (after attempting any sibling independent-marker email) rather than being acknowledged as best-effort, because Stripe does not reliably redeliver an already-acknowledged event and there is no other recovery path for a purely-failed send. Non-transactional notify side effects without their own independent recovery marker (operator inquiry alerts, daily digest) stay best-effort.",
  "src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts's checkout.session.completed handler only records a bundle payment when session.metadata has BOTH slug AND a getBundle(tier)-known tier — any new one-off Checkout Session type (e.g. commerce_sku Buy now) must branch on its own metadata marker (e.g. metadata.kind) BEFORE that slug/tier check and `break`, or it silently falls into the else/console.warn branch with no operator notification. Truthy metadata.tier alone is not enough: checkout create already rejects unknown tiers via getBundle, and the webhook must mirror that enum gate before recordLeadPayment / handoff / receipt (unknown/wrong-cased tiers skip like missing slug/tier — session already marked complete; do not 5xx forever). Receipt HTML must escapeHtmlForEmail the tier label too (prefer getBundle(tier).name) — businessName escaping alone is not enough (bundle-webhook-tier-enum-guard).",
  "Stripe Product/Price sync (or any per-item Stripe object creation) must be idempotent via a stable metadata key (see commerceSkuStripeKey) and must never resolve a charge amount from client input — always look up the Price id server-side from persisted state. Any Stripe `list()` lookup used for idempotency-by-metadata-key must paginate via has_more/starting_after (use listAllPages, src/lib/stripe/list-all-pages.ts) — a single 100-item page silently misses matches past page 1 once an account holds more objects than that. A collection/catalog sync should create/update current items only, never auto-archive/delete a third-party object for removed items (orphan archive is operator-triggered only, and sets active=false — never delete).",
  "saveCommerceCatalogAction must call preserveCommerceStripeIds against the existing DB row after normalizing input — never persist a normalized draft that drops stripeProductId/stripePriceId, and never trust client-submitted Stripe ids. enqueueProductionHandoff must call preserveDeployHandoffOperatorFields against the existing handoff before rebuilding for a new stripeSessionId — a Starter→Growth upsell or re-purchase must not wipe operator-set customDomain/vercelProjectId/progressed status/notes (Convex upsert replace and SQLite ON CONFLICT both clear omitted optionals by default).",
  "Any action that reads a whole-row record, then performs a real, materially long async operation (a sequential per-item external API loop, e.g. syncCommerceCatalogToStripe) before writing that record back via an unconditional full-row upsert (no version/CAS check) has a TOCTOU lost-update window — a concurrent write from a second tab or a follow-up edit during that window gets silently discarded. Re-read the record immediately before the final write and merge in only the fields the long operation actually produced (see syncCommerceCatalogToStripeAction's preserveCommerceStripeIds(freshItems, synced) — the same merge helper saveCommerceCatalogAction uses, with previous/base roles swapped) rather than writing the pre-operation snapshot verbatim. This is a distinct bug class from the money-separation/webhook-idempotency rules above — it's about ordinary operator concurrency on non-webhook writes, not Stripe redelivery. The same window exists when the 'write' at the end is a third-party API mutation rather than a DB row — archiveOrphanCommerceStripeProductsAction's listOrphanCommerceStripeProducts (paginates every active Stripe Product on the account) computes its orphan set from one stale catalog snapshot, then sequentially archives every match; re-read the catalog between the list and the actual mutation step and drop any candidate that reappeared (see orphan-archive-lost-update-guard, archiveCommerceStripeProducts split out for this). Deploy handoff assign/enqueue paths have the same window: tryProvisionVercelDomain is a real Vercel Domains API fetch, then insertDeployHandoff upserts the whole row — re-read + mergeProvisionOntoFreshHandoff before that final write so a concurrent Mark live / Save domain / Assign project survives (see handoff-provision-lost-update-guard). A related status-regression class: updateDeployHandoffStatus always patches status + notes; the bundle webhook's receipt/DNS sent-marker path passes a pre-Resend handoff.status snapshot, so a concurrent Mark live during Resend can be overwritten back to awaiting_dns — fix inside the write (preferAdvancedHandoffStatus never-downgrade on Convex + SQLite, handoff-email-marker-status-no-downgrade), not only by re-reading at the call site (still racy). Before adding a new 'diff current vs desired external state, then mutate' action, check whether the diff snapshot and the mutation step can drift apart during a real (not one-tick) window.",
  "A durable recovery marker (bundleReceiptEmailSentNote / bundleDnsHandoffEmailSentNote / commerceOrders.clientReceiptEmailSentAt, or any future twin) must only be written after checking the resolved `{ success }` shape of the send it gates — never right after an `await` with just a try/catch. sendReceiptEmail / sendEmail / sendCommerceBuyNowClientReceipt (src/lib/email/resend.ts) never throw: a marketing-suppression hit (when skipSuppression is unset) or a Resend API/network failure resolves `{ success: false, reason/error }`. Writing the marker unconditionally after such an await marks a suppressed or failed send as 'sent' forever and permanently defeats the redelivery-recovery path (planBundleWebhookSideEffects / planCommerceWebhookSideEffects). Commerce client receipt must stay independently recoverable from the operator inquiry (clientReceiptEmailSentAt), not gated solely on inquiry presence — see commerce-client-receipt-redelivery-recovery.",
  "Post-payment transactional emails must pass skipSuppression: true into sendEmail (sendReceiptEmail, bundle DNS handoff send in the Stripe webhook, sendCommerceBuyNowClientReceipt) — a cold-outreach CAN-SPAM unsubscribe must not block receipt / DNS go-live / Commerce order-notification after money is taken. Marker-only gating (leave sent-marker unwritten on suppress) is not enough: Stripe redelivers forever against the same suppress. The same rule applies to Starter sold contact-form CRM: POST /api/contact/[slug]'s business/operator inquiry alert AND sendInquiryReply (operator Reply to the visitor) must pass skipSuppression: true — a marketing unsub must not silence website inquiry delivery or leave an inquiry marked replied while the visitor never gets the reply (inquiry-reply-skip-marketing-suppression). Sold Growth/Complete pack delivery on lead.email is the same class: sendMarketingPackToClientEmail and sendClientVoicePackToClientEmail must pass skipSuppression: true (marketing-voice-pack-skip-suppression) — a cold-outreach unsub must not permanently block paid monthly marketing content or Complete phone scripts the client is billed for. After shipping a skipSuppression rule for one product surface, the next empty-backlog scan should check the matching CRM-loop sibling (notify ↔ reply) and other sold lead.email notifies before inventing an optional aggregate. Keep suppression for outreach Email 1–3, abandoned recovery, and Starter upsell. sendOperatorDailyDigest already bypasses suppression for the operator inbox.",
  "Sold contact-form notify and CRM persist must share one soft-capped field set (normalizeContactFormFields / MAX_CONTACT_* in src/lib/inquiries/contact-fields.ts) — never build the skipSuppression notify HTML from uncapped request bodies while insertClientInquiry truncates message (or other fields) for storage. Rate limits blunt request count, not payload size; escapeHtmlForEmail prevents XSS but expands size. Soft-cap once before persist+notify (and again inside insertClientInquiry for webhook writers). After closing a sold-notify skipSuppression hole, the next empty-backlog scan should check notify↔persist field-length parity on that surface before inventing an optional aggregate.",

  // --- Security / injection ---
  "Every dynamic value interpolated into an outbound email's HTML body must go through escapeHtmlForEmail (src/lib/inquiries/reply.ts) — subject lines and From display names are plain text and exempt. That includes href attribute values (previewUrl / checkoutUrl / catalogUrl / unsubscribeUrl / baseUrl + /privacy in resend.ts): sibling templates (client-receipt, marketing/voice deliver-pack, dns-email, daily-digest) already escape URLs; a quote or ampersand in NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL must not break out of href=\"...\". Any value embedded via dangerouslySetInnerHTML into a <script> tag (JSON-LD) must instead go through safeJsonLdScript (src/lib/seo.ts) — JSON.stringify alone does not escape a literal `</script>` breakout, and escapeHtmlForEmail is the wrong tool there. Treat every lead field as untrusted at each render/send sink (it can originate from CSV import), not only at persistence.",
  "Any public link that triggers a state-changing GET/POST keyed only by an email or slug (no session) needs a signed token (see src/lib/email/unsubscribe-token.ts) before assuming persistence/escaping/error-path coverage is sufficient authorization. Public write routes (contact form, Stripe/commerce checkout create, preview-open beacon, unsubscribe) share checkRateLimit (src/lib/security/rate-limit.ts). Auth-gated /api twins (bootstrap-prices, marketing/generate) still need the same per-IP limiter: when OPERATOR_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD is unset, requireOperatorAuth returns ok for every caller — auth alone is not a throttle. loginOperatorAction is also a public unauthenticated password-check — throttle it with a tighter window (5 attempts/60s, key login:<clientIp>) before tryCreateSessionFromPassword; rate-limited attempts redirect to error=rate_limited (never 429 JSON) so the login page can render a clear alert.",
  "Rate limiting isn't only for write-abuse prevention — a public, unauthenticated, data-driven GET surface with no existence short-circuit (any slug resolves to a real-or-synthetic record) and an expensive per-request computation (a DB round trip plus a satori/ImageResponse render, an LLM call, etc.) needs the same per-IP checkRateLimit before that DB lookup, or a scripted loop over random slugs forces unbounded compute/cost with zero throttle — a resource-exhaustion vector distinct from the write-integrity concern the rule above covers. This class is easy to miss because Next.js file-convention routes (opengraph-image.tsx / twitter-image.tsx, and any future sitemap.ts / robots.ts / route segment that reads per-record data) don't look like a route.ts and receive no Request object — derive clientIp via `clientIpFromHeaders(await headers())` from next/headers instead (see preview-og-image-rate-limit, src/app/preview/[slug]/opengraph-image.tsx + twitter-image.tsx). On a rate-limited hit, prefer falling back to a cheaper, already-existing generic response (e.g. the site-wide OG image) over erroring, so a throttled crawler still gets a valid result.",
  "src/proxy.ts custom-domain routing (getLeadSlugByCustomDomain) must stay scoped to an exact pathname === \"/\" rewrite gated behind isPlatformHost — /api/* and /dashboard/* must never be Host-diverted. Preview-subdomain routing (extractPreviewSubdomainSlug) must run BEFORE that custom-domain check, and only rewrite when the extracted label resolves to a real lead — never fall through to the demo-catalog preview for an unrecognized subdomain. Always re-check auth inside server actions; the proxy gate alone is not enough.",
  "Any client-supplied redirect/return/callback URL that gets handed to a third-party API (e.g. Stripe return_url) or used for navigation must be validated same-origin (or same-app relative path) before use, not merely escaped or rate-limited — a public route keyed only by a leakable id (session id, slug) is still an open-redirect vector even with rate-limiting, since one request is enough. Mirror safeNextPath (src/lib/auth/safe-next-path.ts — used by both the login page already-authenticated redirect AND loginOperatorAction; never a weaker startsWith('/') twin that accepts //evil) / isAcceptableBillingPortalReturnUrl (src/lib/billing/return-url.ts): reject on parse failure, origin mismatch, OR over MAX_BILLING_PORTAL_RETURN_URL_LENGTH and fall back to a derived default, never trust the raw value. Optional redirect fields fall back on over-length (hard-reject would strand the primary action; soft-cap would change the destination) — opaque lookup keys still hard-reject 400. Dashboard-only relative next paths also reject backslash host tricks, control chars, scheme-smuggled paths, `..` segments, and /dashboard/login self-loops — including browser-normalized equivalents (hash, `./`, percent-encoded path segments). Apply prefix/login checks to the normalized pathname, not only the raw string.",
  "The appUrl fallback derived from x-forwarded-host/host request headers (when NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL is unset) is itself untrusted input, not just the client-body returnUrl fields the rule above covers — a raw HTTP client or misconfigured proxy can put arbitrary text in those headers. Use resolveTrustedAppUrl / tryHeaderDerivedAppOrigin (src/lib/security/app-url.ts): only host-only http(s) origins are accepted (reject non-http(s) x-forwarded-proto, userinfo, path/query/hash smuggled into Host) and fall back to the localhost default on anything else — a bare `new URL()` success is not enough (`javascript://host` parses). Never inline the `${proto}://${host}` pattern again. Any dynamic value interpolated into outbound email HTML must still go through escapeHtmlForEmail regardless of provenance (env var, header-derived, or otherwise) — see app-url-header-injection-guard + redirect-url-validator-hardening.",
  "Unauthenticated Stripe money redirects (Checkout success_url / cancel_url, Billing Portal return_url — including openBillingPortalAction) must use resolveStripeRedirectAppUrl, NOT resolveTrustedAppUrl and NOT `new URL(request.url).origin`. Precedence is NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL > VERCEL_URL > localhost; never request Host/origin. A Host-spoofed comparison origin bypasses isAcceptableBillingPortalReturnUrl and turns post-payment redirects into phishing — see stripe-redirect-app-url-no-host-trust.",
  "When a route.ts and a dashboard server action are documented as 'twins' sharing one gate (e.g. bootstrapStripePricesAction / POST /api/stripe/bootstrap-prices), verify both actually call requireOperatorAuth — proxy.ts only gates /dashboard/*, never /api/*, so an API route is never auth-protected by the dashboard's route gate alone. An env-flag gate (ALLOW_STRIPE_BOOTSTRAP and similar) is not a substitute for requireOperatorAuth: it's deployment-wide and often set temporarily on an already-authenticated deployment, not a per-caller check. Mirror the requireOperatorAuth-in-a-route-handler precedent at src/app/api/marketing/generate/route.ts.",
  "Any server-side secret comparison (Convex requireApiSecret, operator password, signed-token verification) must be constant-time, not `===`/`!==` on raw strings — a naive compare leaks a byte-at-a-time timing side channel. src/lib/auth/operator.ts's passwordsMatch already does this via node:crypto's timingSafeEqual, but convex/lib/apiSecret.ts cannot reuse that import: it's pulled into every convex/*.ts query/mutation module, and Convex's default runtime only permits Node built-ins in files marked `\"use node\"` — a directive that is action-only and rejected at deploy time on query/mutation builders. requireApiSecret instead uses a hand-rolled constantTimeStringEqual (walks the full max(a.length, b.length) with XOR-accumulate, never short-circuits) — reach for that pattern, not a Node crypto import, for any future secret check inside a convex/*.ts query or mutation file (convex-api-secret-constant-time-compare).",
  "Content-Security-Policy is set per-request from src/proxy.ts (src/lib/security/content-security-policy.ts), not statically from next.config.ts's headers() — a sitewide nonce'd CSP set there would permanently block Next's own framework hydration script on every statically-generated route (marketing/docs pages), since a per-request nonce can never reach HTML that was pre-rendered at build time. isNonceCspPath's allowlist (/dashboard, /checkout, /preview) is the only place that decides which routes get the strict nonce-scoped script-src/style-src tier; every other route gets a nonce-free but still-hardened tier (object-src/base-uri/form-action/frame-ancestors, no script-src/style-src restriction). A new route that Next renders dynamically per request (an operator surface, a payment flow, anything with real auth or PII) needs an explicit addition to that allowlist — it does not inherit the strict tier automatically, and the default (static-safe) tier does not restrict script-src at all. A proxy rewrite must classify by its destination path, not the original request pathname — the preview-subdomain/custom-domain rewrites in src/proxy.ts both land on /preview/[slug] even when the inbound path was \"/\", which is itself in the static-safe tier when not rewritten (csp-nonce-rollout). buildNonceContentSecurityPolicy's default-src 'self' implicitly governs every fetch directive with no explicit override (frame-src included, per the CSP spec's fallback chain) — any cross-origin resource this app embeds (the /preview Google Maps iframe needed an explicit frame-src; a future embedded widget/video/payment iframe would need the same) must get its own directive added at the same time its markup ships, not discovered later as a silent breakage. Verifying a CSP change via curl (header presence, nonce-attribute distribution across <script> tags) does not catch this class of regression — curl never enforces CSP or renders an iframe; a CSP-touching change needs an actual browser check (dev tools console for CSP violation reports, or a scripted browser via Playwright) of every page whose CSP tier changed, not just a header diff (csp-nonce-preview-maps-frame-src).",
  // --- Testing & verification ---
  "For database-touching changes, `npm run build`/`lint` is not enough — run `npm run dev` and load /dashboard/leads and /preview/[slug] before claiming done. Run `npm test` (Vitest) alongside build/lint too — it has caught real breakage build+lint missed. Every payment-critical orchestration module (checkout/commerce-checkout/webhook/billing-portal/bootstrap-prices routes, every dashboard server action, stripe-sync, deploy handoff) has direct unit or route-level coverage — follow the nearest existing sibling test file as the template for a new one rather than relying on an indirect smoke test. Vitest cases that call getClientVoiceGenerator / getMarketingGenerator (or any factory that prefers a live LLM when ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set) must clear/restore that key around the call — generators.test.ts already does this in afterEach; faq-pack.test.ts must match (see client-voice-factory-test-env-isolation). Scheduled agents and CI often inject the real secret, and an unisolated .generate() hits the live Messages API mid-suite.",
  "A `\"server-only\"` lib file cannot be Vitest-tested directly, and its exports must not be re-exported through a barrel that a `\"use client\"` file imports — both break at import/test time. Prefer the pattern in src/lib/commerce/stripe-sync.ts: take the third-party client (Stripe, etc.) as a parameter behind a minimal structural interface, so the logic itself needs no `\"server-only\"` guard and stays unit-testable with a fake client; keep the real client construction + env-var gating only in the server action/route that calls it.",
  "`\"use server\"` files may export ONLY async functions — a `export type` re-export (even one that looks inert) throws `ReferenceError` at module evaluation under Turbopack's server-action transform and 500s every action in that file. Import types from their source module on the client instead. Inline `export type` aliases used only as local return annotations must also drop the `export` keyword (use-server-export-type-cleanup) — they are not a re-export, but they still violate the async-fn-only surface and can become a Turbopack footgun as the transform evolves.",
  "A server action with multiple `return { success: true/false, ... }` statements and no explicit return-type annotation only gets real discriminated-union narrowing on `if (res.success)` when every `success` literal is written `as const` (see markCommerceOrderPaidOutAction) — otherwise TS widens each to `boolean`, `if (res.success)` narrows nothing, and a field only present on the success branch (e.g. a returned id/slug) type-checks as possibly-undefined even though it's always present at runtime, silently defeating the type system's ability to catch a caller that forgets to use it. The same rule applies to send helpers that return `{ success }` shapes (sendEmail / sendOperatorDailyDigest). After any merge that touches `src/app/dashboard/**/actions.ts` or `src/lib/email/resend.ts`, grep for bare `success: true` / `success: false` without `as const` before claiming done — a large fold can silently reintroduce pre-fix literals (see server-action-as-const-success-restore after PR #231). React client components have no test harness in this repo (no @testing-library/react) — extract event-handler logic that touches local list/array state into a pure function beside the component's existing pure-helper module (e.g. src/lib/leads/notes.ts) rather than leaving it untested inline; this is how leads-client-optimistic-state-desync got direct Vitest coverage without adding a new test stack.",

  "stripe-core-publishable-key-false-requirement done: assessStripeCoreEnvReadiness no longer requires NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY — grepped the repo for loadStripe / @stripe/stripe-js and found zero call sites; both checkout flows redirect to a server-created Stripe Checkout Session URL, so the publishable key is never read. The old requirement made StripeCoreEnvBanner show a false alarm even when the two secrets that actually matter (STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET) were both live. Updated launch-checklist/architecture/.env.example to match.",
  "Before adding or trusting an existing 'required env var' readiness banner, grep for actual runtime usage of that var — not just its presence in docs/.env.example/another readiness helper. A var can be a dead requirement (no code path reads it) even after prior audits treated it as load-bearing; that false-'required'-signal bug is distinct from (and just as much launch friction as) the more common missing-readiness-signal case this log otherwise tracks.",

  // --- Product data-model invariants ---
  "Marketing packs persist one row per lead per UTC calendar month (regenerating replaces that month); client voice/FAQ packs persist one row per lead, not month-keyed (regenerating replaces) — do not conflate the two persistence models. Commerce catalogs persist one row per lead (not monthly, cap MAX_COMMERCE_SKUS=50); regenerating replaces the whole catalog. Email pack / Email voice pack both require an existing pack row (current UTC month for marketing) before sending — never auto-generate on send.",
  "Never dump commerce Buy now Checkout Sessions into bundle abandoned-recovery without kind=commerce_sku — Finish-checkout emails pitch Local Site Studio plans and would misfire at end customers. Bundle recovery filters isBundleCheckoutSession; commerce uses listCommerceAbandonedCheckoutsDue + its own preview#catalog recovery path. Commerce Buy now client receipt emails go to lead.email (the local business), never the end-customer shopper — and never reuse sendReceiptEmail (the Local Site Studio bundle buyer receipt).",
  "Starter upsell eligibility must reuse orderEligibleForBillingPortal as the negative check (Billing vs Upsell are mutually exclusive on the same order set) — never invent a parallel 'has recurring' predicate. isFollowUpDue must exclude snoozed leads (a passive queue must never resurface a lead the operator explicitly parked); isColdLead deliberately does NOT exclude snoozed leads, since Re-engage/Close is a valid explicit single-lead override that itself clears the snooze — check whether a new predicate feeds a passive queue or gates an explicit override before blanket-adding a snooze check.",
  "Homepage/pricing CTAs may omit a lead slug — POST /api/stripe/checkout must still put a non-empty metadata.slug on the Checkout Session (resolveBundleCheckoutLeadSlug mints organic-* when missing); never store slug:\"\", since the webhook requires slug && tier and an empty slug permanently drops paid organic buyers from CRM/fulfillment.",
  "When extending the daily digest or pipeline-health with a new order-backed section, load the relevant list* helper once in the existing action/health function and pass it through — do not add a second digest builder or a second dashboard panel for a fact that already ranks in Work next. Digest footnotes for parked urgency (snoozed/cold) must reuse the same pure predicates as pipeline-health, never a parallel due-date/snooze check inside the renderer.",

  "Resend `from` must use EMAIL_FROM via brand.emailFrom / formatResendFromHeader — never hardcode a platform domain (a prior bug sent outreach@updates.localsitestudio.com even after an operator set EMAIL_FROM + SPF/DKIM).",
  // --- Docs & stack ---
  "Ship TS/TSX for product + docs — do not add operator Markdown docs (see AGENTS.md). Use the shared DocPage component for /docs pages. Use Next.js 16 `proxy.ts` (not deprecated `middleware.ts`) for request gating.",

  // --- Current state (as of 2026-07-18) ---
  "All product-facing NEXT_STEPS are shipped; every dashboard server action has direct Vitest coverage; every env var with a launch-blocking silent fallback has an operator-visible readiness banner on /dashboard. The only remaining NEXT_STEPS are operator/legal/infra-gated — five items, not four: stripe-price-objects (run bootstrap-prices), lead-ingestion (Places API, needs legal review), marketing-content-scheduling (Buffer/GBP/Meta OAuth), commerce-payouts-stripe-connect (needs Stripe Connect + counsel), and github-actions-runner-unavailable (GitHub Actions runner/quota misconfiguration blocking pr-checks.yml and every scheduled-agent workflow — operator must check Settings → Actions → General/Runners; not fixable by an agent). Do not fake-complete any of these. When the free backlog is empty, follow the priority order in the cooperation rule near the top of this file (fold a stuck sibling branch > sold-capability wiring gap > Convex/SQLite parity > security/injection audit > untested orchestration > docs clarity) rather than inventing an optional aggregate/metric. This block was consolidated 2026-07-17 from ~35 narrowly-scoped 'after X ships, close Y twin' entries accumulated across the field-length-cap and Stripe entitlement/webhook hardening series (each entry individually correct, but the accumulation had grown AGENT_PROTOCOL — a live rulebook every agent reads before starting work — well past what 'keep this short' calls for), and updated 2026-07-18 to add the fifth (github-actions-runner-unavailable) after several ITERATIONS entries had to manually restate the true count because this block only listed four. The full chronological detail (each specific field/gate/id, in the order it shipped) remains in BUSINESS_DECISIONS + ITERATIONS + git history; this file states only the current, still-binding invariant.",

  // --- Request/field size bounds (public + auth-gated) ---
  "Every write surface enforces one of three size-bound classes, and both twins of a surface — the API route AND its SSR page / Host-header lookup (src/proxy.ts) / dashboard-action counterpart — must enforce the same bound; capping only one side of a twin has been the single most repeated gap in this series. (1) Opaque lookup/session/token ids (Stripe checkout sessionId, vercelProjectId, inquiryId, lead slug, commerce SKU id, unsubscribe token, operator session cookie) hard-reject over a documented max length before any Convex/SQLite/Stripe/Resend call — truncating silently looks up the wrong key or breaks an HMAC verify, so soft-cap/truncate is never correct for this class. (2) Free-text / compliance fields (CSV cells, lead notes, dataSource tag, LLM-generated marketing/voice pack variants, business context joined into a single hours string) soft-cap via a shared normalizer (e.g. normalizeLeadCreateFields, variant-fields.ts) so an oversized value still ships trimmed — cap this at both the write path and any read/deliver path that bypasses that same parser (stub/fallback generators included). (3) Optional attribution/redirect/timestamp fields (billing-portal returnUrl, login next=, preview-open src/utm/openedAt, checkout slug prefill) fall back to a derived default on over-length rather than hard-rejecting the primary action or truncating the destination. Public JSON/form POST routes additionally need a whole-request-body byte ceiling (readJsonBodyWithByteLimit / readFormBodyWithByteLimit) before parsing — per-field caps alone still let a request force unbounded JSON.parse/formData() allocation. requireOperatorAuth is not a payload-size throttle: every dashboard action taking a client-supplied opaque id or bulk text body (e.g. CSV import) needs its own bound, mirroring whatever parser the nearest public-route twin already uses.",

  "Lead slug create-paths normalize to [a-z0-9-]+ via shared sanitizeLeadSlug (src/lib/leads/slug.ts) — collapse non-token runs, strip leading/trailing dashes, AND cap at MAX_LEAD_SLUG_LENGTH=63 (RFC 1035 DNS label max; truncate then re-strip trailing dashes) — used by addLeadAction, CSV explicit-slug column / slugifyBusinessName / collision suffixes, and resolveBundleCheckoutLeadSlug. A create-path that keeps edge dashes (e.g. Demo Cafe! → demo-cafe-) or mints a >63-char label desyncs the CRM slug from leadSlugAsDnsLabel, so suggestedHost is demo-cafe.{root} (or an invalid over-length host) while extractPreviewSubdomainSlug → getLeadBySlug still looks up a different token and misses (Starter preview-subdomain Host→preview falls through). Path *construction* must still encode-at-use via src/lib/leads/slug-path.ts (leadPreviewPath / leadPreviewAbsoluteUrl / leadContactApiPath / encodeLeadSlugPathSegment) — never raw `/preview/${slug}` or `/api/contact/${slug}`. That includes dashboard `revalidatePath` calls: Next matches the path key against the request pathname (which percent-encodes special characters in a single segment), so a raw `/preview/${slug}` with an embedded `/` revalidates the wrong tree. Legacy payment_stub or pre-sanitize rows (and any future create-path miss) otherwise turn `/` into extra path segments. Hostnames cannot percent-encode: suggestedHost uses leadSlugAsDnsLabel (reuses sanitizeLeadSlug so Host→preview identity matches create-path storage, including the 63-cap). Query-string slug values already use encodeURIComponent at checkout links.",
  "markHandoffLive must not call updateLeadStage(..., \"live\") when updateDeployHandoffStatus returns null — a vanished/missing handoff row would leave stage=live while the action returns \"No deploy handoff found\" and paid-not-live / open-handoff metrics diverge (mark-handoff-live-null-status-guard). Mirror the null-check already used by assignVercelProjectAndProvision / setCustomDomainAndProvision after their handoff writes.",
  "Operator Save domain input must be normalized to a bare hostname before persistence (parseOperatorCustomDomain / normalizeOperatorCustomDomainHost in custom-domain-routing.ts) — strip scheme/path/query/hash/port/trailing FQDN dot, reject localhost/single-label/platform-owned hosts, and re-normalize on updateDeployHandoffCustomDomain + getLeadSlugByCustomDomain so a pasted https://client.com/ still equals normalizeRequestHost(Host) and Vercel Domains API name. Never store trim+lowercase alone (custom-domain-host-normalize). assignCustomDomainAction must also reject when getLeadSlugByCustomDomain returns a different lead — Host→preview first-match would silently serve the wrong client (custom-domain-normalize-and-unique).",
  "Custom-domain Host→preview must treat www↔apex as the same sold domain: customDomainHostAliases(host) returns [exact, www-twin] (or [www, apex]); getLeadSlugByCustomDomain tries each alias (exact first); assignCustomDomainAction uniqueness rejects when any alias is owned by another lead; tryProvisionVercelDomain POSTs both aliases when customDomain is set (ignore 409). Keep storing the operator's chosen bare host — do not invent a second normalizer that strips www on write. suggestedHost platform subdomains are not aliased.",
  "OPERATOR_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD must be trimmed on both read and compare: getPassword() / isOperatorAuthConfigured() treat whitespace-only as unset (open demo), and tryCreateSessionFromPassword trims the form-submitted password before passwordsMatch — assessOperatorAuthEnvReadiness already trimmed for the banner, but the live auth path did not, so a trailing space in the env secret or a pasted login field silently locked the operator out while the readiness banner looked fine. Never compare raw env/form strings for this secret (operator-password-trim).",
  "src/proxy.ts dashboardAuthGate must verify OPERATOR_SESSION_COOKIE with the same trimmed getPassword() key that createOperatorSessionToken / requireOperatorAuth use — never raw process.env.OPERATOR_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD. After operator-password-trim, a padded env secret signs sessions with the trimmed value; raw-env verify in the proxy rejects them and infinite-redirects /dashboard/* to login while server actions would accept the cookie (operator-auth-proxy-password-trim / proxy-operator-session-getpassword-parity). Banner + action + proxy are one auth surface; trim any one without the others and the twins drift. Keep path=/ on the session cookie: auth-gated /api twins (bootstrap-prices, marketing/generate) need it.",

  "Public /preview/[slug] must not call listOrders() (full ledger) to decide Commerce catalog visibility — gate on leadStageEligibleForCommerceCatalog first, then use the indexed listOrdersByLeadSlug (convex/orders.listByLeadSlug on by_leadSlug + SQLite WHERE l.slug = ?, registered on the static API map in the same commit) via loadPreviewCommerceItems; a full Convex .collect() / SQLite join on this high-volume path is pure waste that grows with every paid customer (preview-page-uncapped-listOrders). The same indexed helper — not a full listOrders().filter(slug) — is required by every auth-gated per-lead consumer too: createBillingPortalForLead (Manage billing) and the per-lead dashboard eligibility checks (sendStarterUpsellAction, generateMarketingPackAction, sendMarketingPackToClientAction, saveCommerceCatalogAction, generateClientVoicePackAction, sendClientVoicePackToClientAction). Keep full listOrders() only for digest + dashboard ledger-wide aggregates (slug-scoped-listOrders-followthrough).",
  "Never re-export loadPreviewCommerceItems (or any module that imports/lazy-imports `@/lib/db`) from the `@/lib/commerce` barrel — leads-client.tsx (`\"use client\"`) imports that barrel, and Turbopack still pulls `server-only` db into the client graph even when the db import is dynamic, breaking `next build` (PR #313 barrel regression; fixed by importing `@/lib/commerce/preview-commerce` only from Server Components). Same class as the `\"server-only\"` barrel rule above — after shipping a new sold SSR helper that touches db, verify the barrel is not re-exported into a client import path before claiming done (`npm run build`, not just test/lint).",
  "The Convex API map is locked by a structural Vitest guard (src/lib/db/convex-api-map-audit.ts) that scans every convex/*.ts public query/mutation export against the static `const api` map in db.ts and allowlists only the deliberate orphans orders.createOrder / orders.getByStripeSessionId (order writes go through leads.markLeadPaid — no live consumer of those two). A new Convex table/handler needs map registration in the same commit or this test fails; do not widen the allowlist without confirming a new orphan is genuinely deliberate.",
  "Public marketing OUTPUT surfaces (sitemap.xml, /preview/[slug] robots/metadata, JSON-LD) share one indexability predicate — isLeadPreviewIndexable (only curated DEMO_LEADS showcases and paid|live customers) — across sitemap, generateMetadata robots, AND LocalBusiness JSON-LD emission (leadPreviewJsonLdIfIndexable) so they cannot drift; robots=noindex alone is not enough because crawlers still parse structured data. A cold-prospected lead's AI demo must never be invited into a search index under the platform domain without consent.",
  "parseLeadHours must split lead hours on the first '·' only (serializeLeadHours inserts a single separator) — splitting on every middot truncates a weekend value that itself contains '·'.",

  // --- Stripe webhook fulfillment + recurring entitlement ---
  "Every Stripe webhook fulfillment branch (bundle checkout, commerce_sku checkout, subscription lifecycle, invoices, disputes/refunds) must re-derive and re-check the same membership/price-id/currency/amount/payment-status gates the corresponding create-path already enforces via stripe.checkout.sessions.listLineItems — a client-supplied or Dashboard-minted Session/metadata value (tier, commerceAddon, skuId, slug) is never trusted alone. An unknown, mismatched, or ineligible event skips the fulfillment write (the Session/charge is already terminal on Stripe's side — never 5xx forever for a permanently-invalid combination), but any local write failure (ledger insert, entitlement sync, orphan-session cleanup) returns 5xx so Stripe retries.",
  "checkout.session.async_payment_failed must reopen the local checkout_sessions row (mark it open again via markCheckoutSessionOpen, not leave it completed) so Finish-checkout / Buy now abandoned-recovery can nudge a retry — an async ACH failure is not terminal the way a synchronous decline is, and leaving status=complete forever permanently drops that retry revenue. Do not fulfill on async_payment_failed itself.",
  "Recurring MRR entitlement (marketing packs, client voice packs, Commerce catalog + Buy now) is tracked centrally on orders.recurringCanceledAt and kept in sync from every subscription/invoice lifecycle event (created/updated/deleted, pause_collection, past_due/unpaid, payment_failed/paid) by re-proving live subscription items against create-path catalog Price ids — never by Stripe status alone, since status stays active/trialing while collection is paused. Every consumer of 'is this lead currently entitled' (create-path Buy now / catalog sync, webhook, abandoned-recovery, digest/pipeline-health, dashboard action) must share this one predicate set; a helper that accepts entitlement data as an optional argument must require it at the type level, or a future caller can silently omit it and reopen the pause-blind hole.",
  "Abandoned-checkout-session cleanup (marking a stale open session complete once the customer actually pays, so it stops nudging an already-paid customer) must run only after the durable ledger write it depends on succeeds (never immediately after slug-parse — a Session that later skips fulfillment must leave its sibling open carts in recovery), and must close only sessions genuinely superseded by that payment: bundle carts close by lead slug (one subscriber, sequential purchases); Commerce Buy now carts close by lead + SKU + normalized customer email (many simultaneous shoppers can share a slug) — never blanket-close Commerce carts by slug alone.",
  "Bundle/Commerce order refund and dispute tracking (revenueTotalUsd exclusion, pipeline-health + digest visibility, Commerce payouts panel clawback flag) is visibility- and prevention-only — there is no automated clawback (money recovery from a client who was already paid out stays a manual operator conversation). Commerce Buy now DOES have a Mark-paid-out rejection UI: markCommerceOrderPaidOutAction (src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts) rejects the action once order.refundedAt/disputedAt is set, and leads-client.tsx swaps the button for 'Refunded/Disputed — nothing owed' (or '— claw back from client' once already paid out). Bundle orders have no such UI to reject in the first place, since that ledger (orders table) is never mechanically disbursed via a dashboard action at all — see /docs/architecture for the full distinction. Any new dispute/refund lifecycle event needs both its 'opened' and 'closed/reversed' twin shipped in the same pass (see refund.updated / clearRefunded, commerce-order-refund-reversal-tracking), or the flag gets stuck open after Stripe reinstates funds. A reversal/closed-twin handler keyed on a single sub-object EVENT's own status (e.g. refund.updated's per-refund status=failed/canceled) is not ground truth by itself when the parent object can have multiple independent sub-objects — Stripe allows several partial refunds on one charge, so a second refund failing must not blindly clear refundedAt if a different, earlier refund on that same charge genuinely succeeded. Re-fetch the parent (stripe.charges.retrieve) and re-check its own aggregate field (chargeHasRefund's amount_refunded, the same predicate charge.refunded already uses) before clearing — never trust the single event's status alone (refund-reversal-charge-aggregate-recheck).",
  "Any customer-facing notice tied to a paused/failed billing state (e.g. invoice.payment_failed's sendPaymentFailedNotice) needs its restore/recovered twin shipped alongside it — same class as the dispute/refund opened/closed rule above, but for outbound email rather than a dashboard flag. sendPaymentRecoveredNotice (invoice.paid) only fires when clearOrdersRecurringCanceledByCustomer actually cleared a prior pause (cleared > 0) — an ordinary healthy renewal's invoice.paid must stay silent, or every subscriber gets a spurious 'service resumed' email every billing cycle (payment-recovered-confirmation-notice). pause_collection is a distinct axis from invoice.payment_failed/invoice.paid — it fires no invoice event at all, so those two notices never see it. customer.subscription.updated's handler sends sendSubscriptionPausedNotice/sendSubscriptionResumedNotice (deliberately separate copy from the payment-failed pair — 'your card was declined' is false when collection was paused, not a failed charge) gated on Stripe's own event.data.previous_attributes actually containing a `pause_collection` key for this specific event (not merely 'pause_collection is currently set/unset') — this both stops re-notifying on every unrelated subsequent subscription.updated while a pause persists AND stops double-emailing an ordinary past_due/unpaid pause/restore that the invoice.* pair already covers (subscription-pause-collection-notice).",

  // --- Pipeline-health / daily digest aggregation ---
  "Pipeline-health/digest aggregates that sound related can read disjoint tables or predicates (e.g. openHandoffs reads deploy_handoffs.status while paidNotLive reads leads.stage — a lead promoted without a handoff row is invisible to the former). Before assuming a new aggregate is already covered by a sibling, check what table/predicate it actually reads. Every fulfillment/money due-queue on the summary-card grid carries an 'oldest Xh' staleness signal on both dashboard and digest; for a calendar-cadence queue (month/week-keyed), anchor age to the later of the cadence boundary and the record's createdAt, never raw tenure alone, or a long-lived subscriber's queue looks perpetually fresh every reset.",
  "A digest section's displayed rows are capped (5) for email readability, but its header count must come from the pre-slice filtered array's `.length`, never the sliced/displayed array's `.length` — otherwise the header count silently freezes at the cap forever once a queue naturally exceeds it.",
  "The operator dashboard LANDING page (src/app/dashboard/page.tsx) is a deliberately compact pre-drill-down summary, not a second copy of PipelineHealthPanel (src/components/dashboard/pipeline-health-panel.tsx, ~20 MetricCards on /dashboard/leads) — it links out via 'Full funnel breakdown on Lead pipeline' precisely so it doesn't need every due-queue the detailed panel tracks. PRs #466-#469 closed a real gap (the landing page was missing staleness signals, Buy now carts, and Paid-not-live entirely) and PR #470 added the two commerce refund/dispute clawback cards (realized-loss dollar figures, not queue counts — the second-most severe money signal after Revenue itself). That closes the series: do NOT keep mechanically porting every remaining PipelineHealthPanel card (cold leads, follow-ups due, Starter upsell, marketing/voice packs due, commerce catalogs missing/unsynced, commerce orders owed, bundle orders refunded/disputed, recurring entitlement paused, snooze remaining, stage funnel chart, etc.) to the landing page one iteration at a time — that is exactly the 'invent a new optional aggregate just to have something to ship' anti-pattern this file already warns against, just executed one card at a time instead of all at once. A future landing-page addition needs an affirmative case for why it belongs on the *pre-drill-down* summary specifically (e.g. a realized-loss dollar figure on par with the clawback cards), not merely 'PipelineHealthPanel already has this and the landing page doesn't.'",

  // --- Empty-backlog exhaustion (added 2026-07-20 after 5 consecutive clean audits; diff-gate added 2026-07-20 after 8+) ---
  "As of empty-backlog-diff-gate (2026-07-20), the standard empty-backlog priority order (fold a stuck sibling branch > sold-capability wiring gap > Convex/SQLite parity > security/injection audit > untested orchestration > docs clarity) has returned a genuinely clean result across 8+ iterations today alone (see the tail of ITERATIONS — do not hand-recount this in a future entry, the exact number is a distraction; what matters is the trend). Every `*_CONVEX_PRIMARY` constant now has a dedicated unit test; every public GET/write route and every external-API-calling dashboard action is rate-limited; every dangerouslySetInnerHTML site routes through safeJsonLdScript; every outbound-email interpolation is escaped; every dead-code sweep comes back empty; docs pages are current. Both tier-6 candidates named by the prior version of this entry have already shipped (commerce-payouts-stripe-connect-express-account-scaffold + stripe-connect-panel-onboarding-gate-wiring) and tier-7 (docs staleness) has already had a real pass (cli-agents-doc-outage-caveat) — do not re-target either as if still open; identify a fresh candidate at whichever tier applies.",
  "Waste-reduction rule (the actual fix, not just an observation): before dispatching a full tiered research sweep, `git log --oneline <last-verified-clean-sha>..HEAD -- src convex .github/workflows` (last-verified-clean-sha = the merge commit of the most recent ITERATIONS entry whose summary reports a full clean sweep across all five tiers, findable via its cited PR #) excluding prompt_process.tsx itself. An empty result means no product-affecting file has changed since that verified-clean check, so a full re-sweep can only re-derive an identical conclusion — skip straight to a fast 2-3-sample spot-check (not a full research-agent dispatch) and either find a tier-6/7/8 candidate or log a short 'confirmed still clean, zero relevant diff since <sha>' entry. Reserve a full tiered sweep (own read + dispatched research agent) for when that diff is non-empty, since a real code change is exactly what could have introduced a fresh gap. This does not license skipping baseline verification (tsc/lint/test/build) every iteration — those are cheap and catch real regressions the diff-gate doesn't (e.g. a dependency bump); it only gates the expensive from-scratch tier 1-5 research sweep.",
  "This file itself (src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx) passed ~2.9MB / 16,000+ lines as of 2026-07-20 and a plain whole-file Read now hard-fails for any agent whose file-reading tool enforces a size ceiling (Claude Code's Read tool errors above 256KB) — confirmed firsthand this iteration. Do not assume every sibling agent can open this file directly: use `grep -n '\"^export const\"'` to find the AGENT_PROTOCOL / NEXT_STEPS / BUSINESS_DECISIONS / ITERATIONS section boundaries, then `sed -n '<start>,<end>p'` (or your tool's offset/limit read) to pull only the section you need — AGENT_PROTOCOL is small and always safe to read whole; NEXT_STEPS' todo/in_progress items are cheaply isolated via `grep -n '\"todo\"\\|\"in_progress\"'` plus a few lines of context; BUSINESS_DECISIONS/ITERATIONS only need their last few entries read for context, via `tail`/`sed` on the line range, not the whole array. This is a read-tooling workaround, not a fix — archiving or splitting the file would violate the append-only / never-rewrite-prior-entries rule above and risks breaking every concurrent agent's import contract mid-swarm, so it is deliberately out of scope for a single iteration; flagging it here so a future iteration with a concrete, low-risk migration plan (and enough of the swarm quiesced to land it safely) can pick it up rather than rediscovering the same Read failure from scratch (process-log-file-size-read-limit).",
] as const;

