      "An empty-backlog audit subagent dispatched through the cooperation-priority fallback tiers found that this notice, unlike its already-correctly-gated siblings, was keyed only on Stripe's event.data.previous_attributes containing a pause_collection key — which is baked into the event payload and stays true on every redelivery of the identical event, not just the first delivery. Stripe redelivers an event verbatim (same event.id, same previous_attributes) when the endpoint is slow or errors before returning 200, for up to 3 days. markOrdersRecurringCanceledByCustomer's own write is correctly idempotent (its SQL/Convex WHERE clause only touches rows where recurringCanceledAt is still unset, so a replay updates 0 orders), but nothing downstream consumed that idempotency signal — so a slow-but-eventually-successful first delivery whose durable write completed before the response timed out would double-email the customer 'your billing is now paused' on Stripe's automatic redelivery. Fixed by threading the write's own row-count through to the notice gate, the same pattern already proven correct on the resume/recovery side, rather than inventing a separate dedup marker. Deliberately left invoice.payment_failed's dunning notice (recurring-payment-failed-dunning-notice) unchanged: its existing code comment documents that Stripe fires a distinct invoice.payment_failed event per Smart Retry attempt by design, so it intentionally resends once per attempt with no dedup marker — applying an `updated > 0` gate there would regress that intended behavior, since a legitimate second/third retry attempt's markOrdersRecurringCanceledByCustomer call correctly returns 0 (the order was already paused by the first attempt in the same dunning sequence), and gating on it would silently drop the notice for a real subsequent failed charge. Added a redelivery-specific test (`markOrdersRecurringCanceledByCustomer.mockResolvedValueOnce(0)` against an event whose previous_attributes still shows pause_collection as changed) asserting sendSubscriptionPausedNotice is not called and getOrdersByStripeCustomerId is not even reached, alongside the existing 'newly set' happy-path test which continues to pass since the default mock resolves 1. Verified: npm ci (fresh, node_modules not preinstalled) + npm test (2104 passed, 165 files = prior 2103 baseline + 1 new) + npm run lint (clean) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run build (tsc clean, all 31 routes generated) + npm run dev sanity check: curled /, /dashboard/leads, /preview/demo-cafe, /docs/process (all 200), then stopped the dev server and confirmed port 3000 was freed.",
