    decision:
      "splitCsvIntoLines and parseCsvLine (src/lib/leads/csv-import.ts) now only enter quote mode when a `\"` is the FIRST character of a field — immediately after a comma delimiter or at the very start of a row — tracked via a new `atFieldStart` boolean in both functions, reset to true after every comma/newline and false after any other character is consumed. A `\"` encountered when `atFieldStart` is false is now appended as a literal character instead of toggling `inQuotes`. Both functions' doubled-quote (`\"\"`) escape handling inside an already-open quoted field is unchanged.",
    rationale:
      "A dedicated research subagent (run concurrently with the commerce-draft-items-cross-lead-clobber audit this same iteration) mechanically traced both CSV parsing functions character-by-character against RFC 4180 §2(5) and found neither ever checked field position before toggling quote mode — any `\"` anywhere in a field flipped `inQuotes`, whether or not it was actually opening/closing a properly-quoted value. Independently re-verified before fixing (not the subagent's report as ground truth) by writing a standalone reproduction of the *pre-fix* logic and running it directly: for the two-row CSV `businessName,category,city\\n72\" TV Mounting Pros,electronics,Denver\\nSecond Business,salon,Boulder`, splitCsvIntoLines returned only 2 lines instead of 3 — the single stray `\"` after `72` left `inQuotes` permanently true from that point on, so the row's own trailing newline was read as embedded-in-quotes and the entire next row (`Second Business,salon,Boulder`) got silently concatenated onto the corrupted line, which parseCsvLine then read as one garbled field; the `Second Business` lead would vanish from the import with the operator only ever seeing 'Imported N leads' and no error. This is a directly plausible business name for hardware/electronics/fencing/tailoring categories (inch/foot marks are ordinary punctuation there — `72\" TV Mounting Pros`, `6' Fence Co`), and CSV import (csv-lead-import) is the only lead-ingestion path this platform has that isn't operator/legal-gated (Places API stays todo pending legal review), making this the primary at-scale lead-volume surface. Fix approach: track field position with a single boolean rather than restructuring around an explicit field/column index, since both functions already loop character-by-character and only need to know 'was the immediately-preceding non-quote transition a field boundary,' which a comma (parseCsvLine) or a comma/newline (splitCsvIntoLines, which also splits rows) already fully determines. Considered requiring the quote to also be immediately followed only by a comma/newline/EOF at field-close (a stricter RFC reading that would reject e.g. `\"quoted\"trailing` as malformed) but kept the existing closing behavior (first unescaped `\"` while `inQuotes` closes the field, whatever follows) since no current test or real CSV export produces that shape and changing close semantics too was unnecessary scope for this fix. New tests: splitCsvIntoLines — a 3-row CSV with a stray mid-field quote in row 2 stays 3 separate lines (not 2 merged); parseCsvLine — `72\" TV Mounting Pros,electronics,Denver` parses as three clean fields with the literal quote preserved in the first, and a control case (`6' Fence Co,\"tall, sturdy\",fencing`) confirms a real quoted field starting right after a comma still opens correctly alongside an unrelated literal apostrophe. Verified: npm test (2124 passed, 166 files = prior 2121 baseline + 3 new, all in csv-import.test.ts) + npm run lint (clean) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run build (tsc clean, all 31 routes generated) + npm run dev sanity check: curled /, /dashboard/leads, /preview/demo-cafe, /docs/process (all 200), then stopped the dev server and confirmed no next-server process remained (port 3000 free).",
