    id: "starter-upsell-billing-portal-mutual-exclusion-restore",
    decision:
      "In src/lib/billing/starter-upsell.ts, leadEligibleForStarterUpsell's 'lead already has Growth/Complete/Commerce' exclusion now checks orders.some(orderEligibleForBillingPortal) directly and unmodified — removed the isLiveRecurringOrder wrapper (orderEligibleForBillingPortal && !isOrderRefunded && !isOrderDisputed) that starter-upsell-exclusion-refund-dispute-aware added. isStarterOnlyOrder (the Starter-order side of the same mutual-exclusion check) is unchanged — a refunded/disputed Starter order still correctly excludes itself as 'nothing live to upsell off of'; only the Growth/Complete/Commerce side of the check reverted.",
    rationale:
      "A dispatched research subagent (money/entitlement-correctness tier of the empty-backlog cooperation priority order, this same routine's third iteration, explicitly steered away from the two bugs already closed earlier in the same run — stripe-webhook-rate-limit-post-verify and commerce-sku-price-resync-metadata-match) found that isLiveRecurringOrder's refund/dispute carve-out broke the mutual-exclusion guarantee this module's own doc comment (and AGENT_PROTOCOL's still-current cooperation rule: 'Starter upsell eligibility must reuse orderEligibleForBillingPortal as the negative check ... never invent a parallel has-recurring predicate') both promise. orderEligibleForBillingPortal (which gates the dashboard's 'Manage billing' button) never excludes refunded/disputed orders, by design — src/lib/orders/recurring.ts's orderGrantsSoldCapabilityEntitlement docstring already documents that 'a Stripe refund or dispute does not always set recurringCanceledAt (the underlying Subscription can still be active while a single charge was returned)', and I independently confirmed in src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts that the charge.dispute.created/charge.refunded handlers call only markOrderDisputed/markOrderRefunded — neither touches recurringCanceledAt or the Stripe Subscription itself. So a lead with a live Starter order plus a separate Growth order that was disputed WITHOUT canceling the underlying subscription satisfied isLiveRecurringOrder=false (dispute set) while orderEligibleForBillingPortal stayed true (tier=growth, ignores dispute) — the dashboard showed both 'Manage billing' AND the Starter-upsell CTA for the same lead simultaneously. If the operator (or an automated cadence) sent that upsell nudge and the customer paid again, the result is two simultaneously active Growth subscriptions billing the same customer monthly — a real duplicate-charge incident triggered purely by an ordinary chargeback that never actually canceled the first subscription, and arguably worse than a missed upsell since it compounds financial harm on a customer who is already actively disputing a charge. The original starter-upsell-exclusion-refund-dispute-aware decision's stated premise ('that order grants zero live entitlement') conflated two different meanings of entitlement: true for FULFILLMENT gating (packs/catalog delivery correctly stop for a disputed order even if the subscription row looks active — orderGrantsSoldCapabilityEntitlement is right to exclude it there), false for 'does the underlying Stripe subscription still exist and get billed' (which is what the Billing-Portal-vs-Upsell mutual exclusion actually needs to track). Independently re-verified against current source before fixing (not the subagent's report as ground truth): read starter-upsell.ts, portal.ts, and orders/recurring.ts end-to-end; confirmed orderHasActiveRecurringEntitlement's own doc comment already states the general design intent ('Canceled subscribers keep stage=paid|live and Billing Portal access') — i.e. even a recurringCanceledAt subscriber keeps Manage billing by design, so the mutual-exclusion check was never meant to look past tier/commerceAddon at all; confirmed the fix doesn't reintroduce the original narrower bug refund-dispute-entitlement-gate closed on isStarterOnlyOrder (a refunded Starter order still can't anchor an upsell — that predicate is untouched). Considered gating on recurringCanceledAt instead of a blanket revert (only treat a refunded/disputed order as non-blocking when the subscription is ALSO actually canceled) — rejected as unnecessary complexity: AGENT_PROTOCOL already mandates reusing orderEligibleForBillingPortal raw, and the dashboard's own Manage-billing button uses the same raw check, so any narrower predicate here would only reintroduce a smaller version of the same dual-CTA risk for a currently-billing-but-recurringCanceledAt-cleared edge case. Tests: flipped the existing 'allows the upsell when the lead's only Growth/Complete/Commerce order was refunded or disputed' case in starter-upsell.test.ts to assert rejection; added a direct cross-check test iterating five orders (plain Growth/Complete/Commerce-addon, plus refunded Growth and disputed Complete) asserting orderEligibleForBillingPortal(order)===true implies leadEligibleForStarterUpsell(...)===false for every one — the dual-CTA regression this bug produced now has direct coverage, not just individual-predicate unit tests that never cross-check each other; flipped the matching action-level fixture in send-starter-upsell-action.test.ts ('allows the upsell when the existing Growth order was refunded' → 'still rejects'). Also updated /docs/architecture (src/app/docs/architecture/page.tsx — appended a new paragraph rather than editing the historical starter-upsell-exclusion-refund-dispute-aware entry, matching this file's own append-only convention) so the rendered doc reflects the reversal instead of describing dead code.",
