    rationale:
      "A dispatched research subagent, briefed on this repo's empty-backlog priority order (fold a stuck sibling branch > wire an already-shipped-but-unwired sold-capability helper > Convex/SQLite parity/idempotency regression > security audit > untested orchestration > docs), was asked to find a helper that's built, tested, and documented as wired into production but has zero real call sites. It found openBundleCheckoutSessionsToClose / openCommerceCheckoutSessionsToClose: both carry docstrings claiming 'Used by markCompleteOpen...ForLead + Vitest', but grep across src/ shows their only real caller is their own test file — db.ts's two functions of that name never import from abandoned-recovery.ts at all and instead hand-roll the equivalent filter a second time (SQLite: raw WHERE clause) while convex/checkoutSessions.ts hand-rolls it a third time (in-memory loop inside the mutation). Before folding this into 'just fix the stale comment,' independently re-verified whether the three independent implementations actually agree, since AGENT_PROTOCOL already flags exactly this failure class ('never ship two scorers/trackers for the same fact'; dual-backend list-order-parity rule) as high-risk. They do not: db.ts's SQLite UPDATE only flips status when kind IS NULL OR '' OR 'bundle' (an allowlist matching openBundleCheckoutSessionsToClose's own isBundleCheckoutSession predicate exactly), but convex/checkoutSessions.ts's markCompleteOpenBundleForLead instead skipped only kind === \"commerce_sku\" (a blocklist) — closing any OTHER kind value too. Confirmed this is not merely a stylistic difference: read convex/schema.ts directly and found checkoutSessions.kind is declared as a loose v.optional(v.string()), not the closed CheckoutSessionKind (\"bundle\" | \"commerce_sku\") union src/lib/checkout/types.ts defines at the TypeScript layer — so a stray value from a bypassed write path, a future third session kind, or manual Convex-dashboard data entry would, on Convex only, get silently auto-closed as if it were a paid-off bundle cart (dropping it out of abandoned-recovery / Finish-checkout nudges) while the SQLite backend would correctly leave that same row open. Latent today (only two kind values exist in practice) but exactly the kind of backend-behavior split this repo's own invariants exist to prevent before it bites — the sibling commerce-side Convex handler (markCompleteOpenCommerceForLeadSkuCustomer) already used the correct allowlist pattern (kind !== \"commerce_sku\" → skip, i.e. requires kind === \"commerce_sku\"), making the bundle-side handler's blocklist an inconsistency within the same file, not just against SQLite. Fixed by rewriting the Convex handler's condition to the same three-way allowlist SQLite already uses, and rewrote both abandoned-recovery.ts docstrings to state the true (spec-only, not call-graph) relationship rather than the false 'Used by X' claim, so a future reader does not repeat the same false assumption the subagent had to disprove by reading db.ts and convex/checkoutSessions.ts directly. New Convex test inserts a row with kind: \"future_kind\" directly via t.run (bypassing the create mutation's closed-union arg validator, matching how a real stray value would have to originate) and asserts markCompleteOpenBundleForLead leaves it open — the SQLite side already had equivalent coverage via its own allowlist WHERE clause reasoning, so no new SQLite-level test was needed to lock in the fix itself, only to prove the two backends now agree on the exact predicate. Did not attempt to collapse the three implementations into one shared runtime function: SQLite's WHERE-clause and Convex's server-side mutation loop are different execution contexts with no code-sharing path today, so keeping abandoned-recovery.ts as an accurately-documented spec (rather than falsely claiming to be an imported dependency) was judged the correct, minimal fix — matching this iteration's scope rather than a larger refactor. Verified: npm ci (fresh) + npm test (2135 passed, 166 files = prior 2134 baseline + 1 new, in convex/checkoutSessions.test.ts) + npm run lint (clean) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run build (tsc clean, all 31 routes generated).",
