      "Extracted a new pure exported helper newClientInquiryId(leadSlug) into src/lib/inquiries/inquiry-id.ts (the id-bounds module already shared by replyToInquiryAction/updateInquiryStatusAction) — `inq_${leadSlug}_${Date.now().toString(36)}_${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8)}`, the same timestamp+random pattern newCommerceOrderId/newCommerceSkuId already use — and switched src/lib/db.ts's insertClientInquiry to call it instead of inlining `inq_${input.leadSlug}_${Date.now()}` directly.",
    rationale:
      "Two independent research subagents were dispatched this iteration through the empty-backlog cooperation priority tiers (all five gated NEXT_STEPS items unchanged; the sole open PR #202 remains a weeks-stale human/Cursor draft, not a fold candidate; a fold scan of the newest sibling claude/* branches by commit timestamp found every one already merged, consistent with every prior same-day iteration). One found insertClientInquiry's id generator was the sole app-owned id generator in this codebase with no random component — unlike newCommerceOrderId (src/lib/commerce/orders.ts) and newCommerceSkuId (src/lib/commerce/catalog.ts), which both append a Math.random() suffix to their base36 timestamp specifically to guard against same-millisecond collisions, insertClientInquiry used plain `Date.now()` (millisecond resolution only) concatenated with leadSlug. This id is the sole lookup key for convex/inquiries.ts's by_inquiryId index, which getById/updateStatus/recordReply all query via `.unique()` — a Convex query method that throws when more than one document matches, rather than returning either row. Two inquiries for the same lead created in the same millisecond therefore mint an identical inquiryId and both writes to convexClient.mutation(api.inquiries.create, ...) succeed (the create mutation inserts unconditionally with no existing-id check), silently producing a duplicate-key pair that permanently breaks every subsequent by-id lookup for both rows. Concrete failure path traced end-to-end before fixing: a client-side double-submit or a retried fetch to the public, only-rate-limited (20/60s) POST /api/contact/[slug] (src/app/api/contact/[slug]/route.ts) landing in the same millisecond as a second contact-form submission for the same lead, OR two near-simultaneous commerce_checkout webhook side-effect inquiries (src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts) for the same lead — both entirely plausible on a real production traffic pattern, not a contrived edge case. Once collided: getClientInquiryById (db.ts) calls Convex getById, which throws on the duplicate `.unique()` match; the `shouldFallbackToSqliteOnConvexFailure` catch routes to SQLite, where NEITHER row exists (both writes went to Convex, never SQLite, since Convex succeeded) — returns null, so updateInquiryStatusAction/replyToInquiryAction report 'Inquiry not found' for what the operator can plainly see in their own inbox list (list/listBySlug/listUnreplied all use `.collect()`, not `.unique()`, so both duplicate rows still render, visibly doubled). Because INQUIRY_REPLY_CONVEX_PRIMARY requires Convex (requireConvex: true), a genuine Convex getById throw on the write side surfaces as a permanent, non-retryable 'Could not update/record — retry in a moment' in the dashboard — retrying never helps, since the duplicate-id collision that caused the throw never resolves itself. Both orphaned rows also permanently inflate unrepliedInquiryCount and the daily digest's Unreplied inquiries section, since neither can ever be marked read/replied/archived again. Confirmed this is not already covered by any existing AGENT_PROTOCOL rule (read all 141 lines): the closest related rules are Stripe webhook idempotency-by-stripeSessionId and Stripe object idempotency-by-metadata-key, neither of which is about an app-generated primary-key generator colliding with itself; this is a distinct bug class (id-generator collision), not a redelivery/retry-idempotency gap. Considered mirroring checkoutSessions.create's existing-row `.unique()` guard-then-skip pattern in convex/inquiries.ts's create mutation as defense-in-depth — deliberately scoped out of this fix: that pattern exists for Stripe-redelivery idempotency (a legitimate reason the SAME stripeSessionId arrives twice and the second write should be a no-op), whereas here two DIFFERENT inquiries colliding on id is purely a generator defect with no legitimate 'skip the second write' semantics — silently dropping a second real inquiry because its id happened to collide would be a worse failure mode than the collision itself, and fixing the generator's actual randomness gap (matching the exact precedent both sibling commerce id generators already establish) removes the collision at its root rather than papering over it downstream. Independently re-verified against current source before fixing (not the subagent's report as ground truth): read src/lib/db.ts:1972 directly and confirmed the exact template literal; read convex/inquiries.ts's create/getById/updateStatus/recordReply and confirmed every one keys off by_inquiryId with `.unique()`; read src/lib/inquiries/inquiry-id.ts and confirmed MAX_INQUIRY_ID_LENGTH=128 comfortably fits the new id shape even at MAX_LEAD_SLUG_LENGTH=63 (verified via a new test: `${'a'.repeat(63)}` slug still parses ok); grepped every `inq_` literal across src/convex for a test or call site that depends on the EXACT pre-fix format (millisecond-decimal Date.now(), no random suffix) rather than merely a leading `inq_` prefix — none found; every existing test either hardcodes a fully literal mock id (`inq_demo_1`, `inq_test_1`) that bypasses the real generator entirely, or already generates its own fixture id independently (pipeline-health.test.ts's own `inq_${Math.random()...}` helper). New tests in inquiry-id.test.ts: newClientInquiryId returns a string prefixed `inq_<leadSlug>_`; 50 same-tick calls for the same leadSlug produce 50 distinct ids (direct regression for the collision — the pre-fix generator would produce far fewer than 50 distinct values run synchronously in a tight loop, since Date.now() resolution is far coarser than a JS loop iteration); a max-length-slug id still parses ok under MAX_INQUIRY_ID_LENGTH via parseInquiryId. Verified: npm ci (fresh) + npm test (2146 passed, 166 files = prior 2143 baseline + 3 new, all in inquiry-id.test.ts) + npm run lint (clean) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run build (tsc clean, all 31 routes generated) + npm run dev sanity check: curled /, /dashboard/leads, /preview/demo-cafe, /docs/process (all 200; grepped the dashboard HTML for error-boundary markers — none), then stopped the dev server and confirmed no next-server process remained (port 3000 free).",
