    rationale:
      "This is the Convex/SQLite-parity/idempotency-tier finding a research subagent surfaced last iteration (commerce-payouts-owed-staleness-signal) and deliberately deferred, since that iteration's sold-capability finding ranked higher per AGENT_PROTOCOL's own empty-backlog priority order; picked it up this iteration as the next-highest free tier once a fresh fold-scan of 370 unmerged remote branches (all candidates with ≤3 commits ahead of origin/main) found zero stuck sibling work — every one of the ~15 individually re-verified (CSV stray-mid-field-quote fix, Stripe Product/Price reactivate-on-resync, Paid-not-live remedy panel, per-lead refund/dispute panel, webhook rate-limiting, deployHandoffs status-rank dedupe, per-lead Commerce-catalogs-unsynced panel, orderHadRecurringEntitlement dedupe, Starter-upsell refund/dispute exclusion, convex-test coverage additions, .nvmrc Node pin, CSV slug-collision mislabeling fix, and — initially looking like a genuine gap until checked against src/lib/seo.ts's already-shipped staticPageMetadata/docPageMetadata — the docs/checkout/legal canonical-URL fix) turned out already shipped on main by an equivalent independent implementation, matching this file's own prior note that this has held for '~20 iterations' running. Verified the bug directly before fixing: `leads.id`/`orders.id`/`checkout_sessions.id` are all SQLite `TEXT PRIMARY KEY` columns (db.ts's CREATE TABLE statements); `checkout_sessions` additionally has `ON CONFLICT(stripeSessionId) DO NOTHING`, which only de-dupes on that one column — a colliding `id` from a *different* stripeSessionId inserted in the same millisecond still throws a constraint-violation error, since SQLite's ON CONFLICT clause only catches a violation on the column(s) it names. Concrete failure scenario: a CSV bulk-import loop (up to MAX_ROWS=100 fresh leads, inserted synchronously in a tight loop via better-sqlite3, which is fast enough to comfortably clear >1000 inserts/sec on commodity hardware) has a real, non-negligible chance of minting two `lead_<sameMs>` ids for two different rows in the same millisecond; the second INSERT throws an uncaught SQLite constraint error, corrupting the whole batch's transaction state for an operator-facing bulk import — the exact same failure class the inquiry-id-collision-guard fix already closed for `client_inquiries.id`, just never fully swept across its three sibling generators despite that entry's own doc comment (mirrored from newCommerceOrderId's) explicitly noting inquiry-id was 'the only app-owned id generator in this codebase with no random component' — a claim this iteration's audit found to already be stale at the time it was written (leads/orders/checkout-session ids were bare Date.now() then too), not merely something that regressed later. Chose per-table generator functions living beside each domain's existing `types.ts` (mirroring newCommerceOrderId's placement in commerce/orders.ts) over one shared generic helper, since a shared `newId(prefix)` would need to trust every call site to pass the right prefix with no type-level guarantee, whereas `newLeadId()`/`newOrderId()`/`newCheckoutSessionId()` are each impossible to call for the wrong table. Explicitly checked (and rejected as out of scope) the two other bare-Date.now() id sites the audit also found (deploy_handoff.ts, dashboard leads actions.ts) — both are safe by construction because the id already embeds the row's own unique key, so fixing them would be manufactured busywork, not a real bug; noted here so a future audit does not re-flag them without re-deriving this same reasoning. Tests: three new describe blocks (leads/types.test.ts, orders/types.test.ts addition, checkout/types.test.ts) each asserting the prefix and a same-millisecond 50-call Set-size collision-guard, mirroring inquiry-id.test.ts's own regression test shape exactly. Verified: npm ci (fresh) + npm test (2167 passed, 168 files = prior 2161 baseline + 6 new, across the three id-generator test files) + npm run lint (clean) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run build (tsc clean, all 31 routes generated) + npm run dev sanity check: curled /, /dashboard/leads, /preview/demo-cafe, /docs/process (all 200), then stopped the dev server and confirmed port 3000 free.",
