    decision:
      "MAX_CSV_IMPORT_TEXT_LENGTH's doc comment (src/lib/leads/csv-import.ts) claimed 512 KiB 'comfortably fits 100 capped rows (create-path MAX_* per cell)'. That's false by roughly 2.6x once MAX_SERVICES=100 x MAX_SERVICE_ITEM_LENGTH=100 is accounted for — a single fully-compliant row can already be ~13.6KB, so 100 such rows need ~1.36MB, not 512KB. A services-heavy bulk import (any of the four BusinessCategory values, but especially trades) hits the byte gate well short of the advertised MAX_ROWS=100, and importLeadsCsvAction returned an unhelpful flat 'CSV text is too long' with no indication why or how to recover. Fixed the doc comment to state the true relationship; changed the too_long error to name the exact character ceiling and suggest splitting into smaller batches; added two Vitest guards (csv-import.test.ts) — MAX_CSV_IMPORT_TEXT_LENGTH pinned under Next's 1MB default serverActions.bodySizeLimit, and a recomputed worst-case-row-bytes assertion so this exact capacity claim can't silently drift false again.",
    rationale:
      "Picked up as a scheduled routine continuing this backlog after PR #498 (update-lead-stage-action-paid-live-revert-guard, tip of main at d3e4239). NEXT_STEPS confirmed fully gated (same five operator/legal/infra-blocked items — stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable), no fold candidates (only origin/main and this branch existed on the remote; the one open PR #202 is the same weeks-stale unrelated draft every prior iteration has noted). Dispatched a research agent on the empty-backlog tier order; its first attempt stalled waiting on a nested sub-agent it had itself spawned and returned nothing useful (a harness quirk, not a finding — burned real budget with zero commit), but the nested sub-agent's own report landed separately and, after tier-1/2/3 sweeps across the Stripe webhook route (setup/verification + checkout.session.completed + invoice/refund/dispute branches), src/lib/payments/refunds.ts + webhook-fulfillment.ts, convex/commerceOrders.ts vs its SQLite twin in db.ts, src/lib/orders/sqlite-row-mapping.ts, proxy.ts + custom-domain-routing.ts, and every convex/*.ts table found nothing new (all correctly idempotent, 5xx-on-failure, and already-audited), surfaced this csvimport capacity-math mismatch. I independently re-verified before touching anything: read create-fields.ts's actual per-field caps, computed the worst-case fully-compliant row by hand (~13.6KB, confirmed against a template CSV row), confirmed MAX_ROWS=100 is enforced as a wholly separate gate on parsed row count (csv-import.ts:347) rather than derived from the byte ceiling, and confirmed via node_modules/next/dist/docs (per this repo's Next-version-drift convention) that next.config.ts does not override serverActions.bodySizeLimit, so Next's own 1MB default already sits above this 512KB ceiling — ruling out 'just raise the constant to fit 100 rows' as a safe fix, since ~1.36MB would exceed that framework limit and turn a clean too_long result into an opaque Next-level rejection for anyone between 512KB and 1MB. Grepped prompt_process.tsx for 'MAX_CSV_IMPORT_TEXT_LENGTH' and '512_000' and confirmed no prior entry re-derives or challenges this exact arithmetic — prior entries only cover adding the gate itself and confirming MAX_ROWS + the byte cap 'exist and are checked before parsing', not whether the byte cap's own stated capacity claim holds.\n\nScope deliberately narrow: this is a documentation/messaging fix plus regression-guard tests, not a behavior change to the reject/accept boundary — soft-cap or truncation was already correctly ruled out by the existing hard-reject-only design (mid-row truncation would mint corrupt leads), and there is no safe way to raise the byte ceiling without also touching next.config.ts's serverActions.bodySizeLimit, which is out of scope for a narrowly-scoped fix. Real remaining exposure (an operator with a large, valid, services-heavy CSV needs to split it into multiple imports) is now at least clearly communicated in the error message instead of silently mysterious.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run src/lib/leads/csv-import.test.ts src/app/dashboard/leads/import-leads-csv-action.test.ts (64 passed, including the 2 new capacity-math guard tests) + npm test (2328 passed, 174 files = 2326 baseline + 2 new) + npm run build (clean, same 34-route static/dynamic split). No database-touching behavior changed (pure message/doc/test edit — parseCsvImportText's ok/too_long/missing branching is unchanged), so this iteration relied on the full test/lint/build/typecheck pass rather than an additional npm run dev pass.",
