      "Picked up as a scheduled routine (\"continue toward a profitable, growing business\") after PR #508 (bootstrap-prices-action-rate-limit, tip of main at b63a265); this branch (claude/upbeat-dijkstra-qypzvd) was already at that exact tip on the remote. Confirmed baseline green: fresh npm ci + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npm test (177 files / 2367 passed) + npm run build (clean, same 34-route split). NEXT_STEPS confirmed still fully gated via direct read (exactly five status=\"todo\" entries, unchanged). Fold-scanned the remote: sorted all ~448 origin/* branches by last-push timestamp; the ~20 most-recent claude/* branches are all same-session siblings already merged into main (verified via git log timestamps against origin/main's own tip). mcp__github__list_pull_requests confirmed the one open PR (#202) is still the same weeks-stale unrelated draft every prior iteration has noted — no live fold candidate.\n\nDispatched a research agent primed with the full AGENT_PROTOCOL text and the tier 1-5 empty-backlog fallback order, told to independently verify any candidate and check the tail of ITERATIONS/BUSINESS_DECISIONS for prior coverage before reporting. It re-read the immediately-preceding iteration's claim (bootstrap-prices-action-rate-limit: \"the one server action in the app that calls a real external paid API... with zero rate limiting\") skeptically rather than at face value, and grepped every dashboard action for a Stripe client construction (`new Stripe(`) cross-referenced against checkRateLimit usage in the same function body. It found three more: openBillingPortalAction (creates a real Stripe Billing Portal Session), syncCommerceCatalogToStripeAction (up to MAX_COMMERCE_SKUS=50 sequential live Stripe Product/Price create-or-update calls per invocation), and archiveOrphanCommerceStripeProductsAction (paginates every active Stripe Product on the whole account via listAllPages, then archives matches) — all three gated only by requireOperatorAuth, which AGENT_PROTOCOL's own security section states explicitly is not a throttle when OPERATOR_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD is unset (the app's documented \"open demo\" mode).\n\nIndependently re-verified before touching anything: read all three functions in src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts end to end and confirmed zero checkRateLimit/clientIpFromHeaders calls in their bodies or in the library functions they call (src/lib/commerce/stripe-sync.ts, src/lib/billing/create-portal-session.ts — grepped both, zero hits); grepped prompt_process.tsx for every existing `*rate-limit*` id and confirmed none references billing-portal (the dashboard action, distinct from the already-rate-limited public POST /api/stripe/billing-portal route), catalog-to-stripe sync, or orphan-archive; confirmed none of the three existing test files (open-billing-portal-action.test.ts, sync-commerce-catalog-to-stripe-action.test.ts, archive-orphan-commerce-stripe-products-action.test.ts) asserted rate-limit behavior. This is the same already-documented twin-parity/auth-alone-is-not-a-throttle class as bootstrap-prices-action-rate-limit, applied to three previously-missed call sites in the same file, not a new bug class and not a re-instance of any already-shipped fix.\n\nShipped: each of the three actions now calls checkRateLimit keyed by clientIpFromHeaders(await headers()) immediately after requireOperatorAuth succeeds, returning the same friendly `{ success: false, error: \"Too many requests — please try again shortly.\" }` shape every sibling rate-limited action already returns. Deliberately used a distinct key prefix per action rather than reusing `billing-portal:` for openBillingPortalAction — that string is already the bucket key for the public, unauthenticated POST /api/stripe/billing-portal route (stripe-webhook-billing-portal-rate-limit), and sharing it would let dashboard-operator traffic silently share rate-limit budget with public self-serve billing-portal traffic on the same IP; used `dashboard-billing-portal:` instead so the two surfaces are independently throttled.\n\nTest coverage: added one rate-limit case per action's existing test file, each mirroring generate-marketing-pack-action.test.ts's shape (exhaust the 20-request/60s DEFAULT_RATE_LIMIT window with successful calls, then assert the 21st returns the friendly error with the Stripe-calling helper and the Stripe constructor never invoked). sync-commerce-catalog-to-stripe-action.test.ts and archive-orphan-commerce-stripe-products-action.test.ts previously mocked next/headers as a bare `vi.fn()` with no resolved value (fine when the action never called headers() itself); both needed a proper header-store mock (matching open-billing-portal-action.test.ts's existing pattern) added in the same commit, since clientIpFromHeaders now calls `.get(...)` on whatever headers() resolves to and a bare `vi.fn()` resolves to undefined, which would have thrown at import/setup time for every test in those two files, not just the new one.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run src/app/dashboard/leads/open-billing-portal-action.test.ts src/app/dashboard/leads/sync-commerce-catalog-to-stripe-action.test.ts src/app/dashboard/leads/archive-orphan-commerce-stripe-products-action.test.ts (38 passed) + npm test + npm run build. NEXT_STEPS: unchanged (all five gated items stayed as-is) — this is a security-hardening fix on already-shipped code, not a new backlog item.",
