    rationale:
      "Picked up as a scheduled routine (\"continue toward a profitable, growing business\") after PR #509 (commerce-stripe-dashboard-actions-rate-limit, tip of main at c495a8c). Confirmed baseline green (fresh npm ci + npx tsc --noEmit clean + npm run lint clean + npm test 2370 passed/177 files + npm run build clean, same 34-route split), local `main` ref re-synced to origin/main (it had gone stale from an earlier checkout — see the AGENT_PROTOCOL warning about diffing against a stale local main). NEXT_STEPS confirmed still fully gated (same five operator/legal/infra-blocked items, unchanged). mcp__github__list_pull_requests confirmed the only open PR (#202) is still the same weeks-stale unrelated draft every prior iteration has noted; the ~30 most-recently-pushed origin/claude/* branches are all already-merged same-session siblings with no live fold candidate.\n\nThe prior two iterations (bootstrap-prices-action-rate-limit, then commerce-stripe-dashboard-actions-rate-limit) both found unthrottled Stripe-calling dashboard actions by grepping `new Stripe(` against checkRateLimit usage in the same function body, and both times explicitly noted the fix generalizes a pattern rather than closing a one-off. That framing — \"any dashboard action that calls a real external paid/reputation-bearing API needs the same per-IP throttle, since requireOperatorAuth alone is not one when OPERATOR_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD is unset (the documented open-demo mode)\" — was never actually carried over to the other real external API this app calls constantly: Resend email sends. Wrote a small script to walk every `export async function *Action` in src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts (28 total) and flag which call a `send*Email`/`sendOperatorDailyDigest`/`sendInquiryReply`/`sendOutreach*` helper with no `checkRateLimit(` in the same function body. Twelve came back positive; manually read every one end to end to rule out false positives from the heuristic (updateInquiryStatusAction and markCommerceOrderPaidOutAction matched the text search but persist only, no send — correctly excluded; snoozeLeadAction/updateLeadNotesAction/importLeadsCsvAction/saveCommerceCatalogAction/getCommerceCatalogAction/createDeployHandoffForLeadAction/markHandoffLiveAction/assignVercelProjectAction/assignCustomDomainAction confirmed to never call a send helper — correctly excluded).\n\nOn an open-demo deployment (OPERATOR_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD unset — a supported config, not a misconfiguration) any caller who knows a lead slug or a leaked checkout-session/inquiry id could hammer these twelve actions in an unthrottled loop: burn Resend send quota/cost, spam a real website visitor's inbox via replyToInquiryAction, spam a real shopper's inbox via the two checkout-recovery actions, or repeatedly force sendDailyDigestAction's dozen full-ledger Promise.all reads (a real per-request compute cost distinct from the send itself, same class as the preview-og-image-rate-limit concern for expensive uncapped GETs). Every one of these twelve already follows persist-then-notify (durable mark before Resend) for its data-integrity contract — that guards correctness under retries, not request volume; it does nothing to bound how many times a caller can trigger the action in the first place.\n\nShipped: each of the twelve now calls `checkRateLimit` keyed by `clientIpFromHeaders(await headers())` immediately after `requireOperatorAuth` succeeds and before any other work, returning the same `{ success: false, error: \"Too many requests — please try again shortly.\" }` shape every existing rate-limited action already uses — copied verbatim from openBillingPortalAction's established placement. updateLeadStageAction, disposeColdLeadAction, and addLeadAction only conditionally send an email (a stage move to outreach_sent, a re-engage with sendEmail1, or a create directly into outreach_sent) but gate the whole action rather than branching the rate-limit check around the conditional send — matching how syncCommerceCatalogToStripeAction and archiveOrphanCommerceStripeProductsAction were already gated as whole actions in the prior iteration, and simpler to reason about than a rate limit that only sometimes applies within the same action.\n\nTest coverage: added one rate-limit case per action to its existing per-action test file (checkout-recovery-actions.test.ts covers two), mirroring the established shape (exhaust the 20-request/60s DEFAULT_RATE_LIMIT window with successful calls reusing the file's existing default-success mocks, then assert the 21st call returns the friendly error with the nearest DB lookup mock never invoked). Nine of the eleven touched test files (checkout-recovery-actions.test.ts plus the ten action-specific files, minus send-daily-digest-action.test.ts which already had a real header-store mock) previously mocked next/headers as a bare `vi.fn()` with no resolved value — harmless while the action never called headers() itself, but clientIpFromHeaders now calls `.get(...)` on whatever headers() resolves to, and a bare `vi.fn()` resolves to undefined and throws at test-setup time. Fixed all nine to the same headersMock + headerStore() pattern open-billing-portal-action.test.ts established, in the same commit as the action change, per the AGENT_PROTOCOL note this exact class of test breakage already left on the prior iteration.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run across all eleven touched test files (193 passed) + npm test (2382 passed, 177 files = 2370 baseline + 12 new) + npm run build (clean, same 34-route ○/ƒ split). Real npm run dev pass: curled /, /dashboard, /dashboard/leads, /preview/demo-cafe, /docs/process (all 200); stopped the dev server and confirmed port 3000 free afterward; deleted local.db. NEXT_STEPS: unchanged (all five gated items stayed as-is) — this is a security-hardening fix on already-shipped code, not a new backlog item.",
